---
term: Integration
aka:
  - Associative consolidation
  - Consolidation
  - Differentiated integration
  - Membership tier
  - Tier
section: life-cycle
order: 52
definition: A party's movement between discrete tiers that a charter defines, from base [[associate]] to full [[member]], in either direction.
seeAlso:
  - status
  - admission
  - centralization
---
A charter's articles are discrete provisions written in language, so the terms of belonging are discrete too. Each tier is a set of articles that the party accepts. A charter can define any number of tiers between base associate and full member, or none.

Integration applies to people and organizations alike, because an individual takes part as a [[personal corporation]]. At the shallow end, a person takes a single paid task. At the deep end, they hold every right and obligation the charter defines. Between charters, the same range runs from a supplier contract to a member subsidiary.

A party can integrate further toward full membership or move back toward associate status. Which dimensions a tier varies, such as voice, protection, exit cost, or commitment, and how a party moves between tiers are choices for each charter's design. The framework sets no limit on how far a party can integrate.

Integration matters most for specialization. Gary Becker distinguished *general* skills, which are useful anywhere, from *specific* skills, which are valuable only inside one organization. General skills don't need integration, because an open market already rewards them. Oliver Williamson calls specific investment *asset specificity*. Once a party has committed such an investment, the other side can renegotiate the terms in its own favor, which economists call the *hold-up problem*. Persistent membership is the traditional protection. Tiers let a charter offer that protection without requiring every party to become a permanent member.

The range from associate to member follows Williamson's continuum of governance structures. The continuum runs from markets, through hybrid forms such as long-term contracts, to full hierarchy. The European Union calls the same pattern among its members and partners *differentiated integration*.

A charter that wants a ready-made set of tiers can use the bundle of rights that Edella Schlager and Elinor Ostrom defined for common-pool resources. Each position holds every right of the one before it and adds one more:

| Position | Right added |
|---|---|
| Authorized entrant | Access |
| Authorized user | Withdrawal |
| Claimant | Management |
| Proprietor | Exclusion |
| Owner | Alienation, which is the right to sell or transfer the other rights |
